; expect 42
; The three keyword-headed expression forms stage0's parser has and
; stage1's did not. Each used to arrive as an application of a bare
; name, so a program using any of them drew a false AX3001 from a
; checker that had no idea they were keywords - and `(struct ...)`
; drew two, since its type name landed where an argument goes.
;
;   (struct Name field...)  construction, distinct from `(Name ...)`
;   (consume e)             transparent: stage0's checker, IR and
;                           `Expr::span()` all see through to `e`
;   (alloc Type [count])    types `*mut Type` and evaluates to 0 -
;                           stage0's IR generator has NO case for it,
;                           so it reaches the generator's catch-all
;
; The `alloc` term is the load-bearing one: it contributes 0, so this
; case fails if stage1 ever "improves" on stage0 by allocating.
(struct P
  (x : Int)
  (y : Int))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (p (struct P 40 2))
    (z (cast Int (alloc Int 8)))
  )
    (+
      (+
        p.x        (consume p.y)
      )      z
    )
  )
)
